<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dara Shikoh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1615">1615</a>–<a href="/years/1659">1659</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Dara Shukoh · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dara Shikoh was the eldest son and favoured heir of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>. A scholar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a>, he translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> into Persian and wrote The Mingling of the Two Oceans on the common ground of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. In the war of succession that began in 1657 his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> defeated him at Samugarh and Deorai, and he was captured and executed in 1659.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh.jpg" alt="Dara Shikoh" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gurkan1526 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dara-S.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad Dara Shikoh was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> on 20 March 1615, the first son and third child of Prince Khurram, the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, and Mumtaz Mahal. He studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, history, Persian poetry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, and beside his native Persian learned Hindi, Arabic and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. He and his elder sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahanara-begum">Jahanara Begum</a> were their father's favourite children.</p><p>On 1 February 1633 he married his half-cousin Nadira Banu Begum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, and he contracted no other marriage. In the same year he was appointed heir apparent, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> formally confirmed on 10 September 1642 with the title Shahzada-e-Buland Iqbal, Prince of High Fortune. He was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> in 1645 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1648.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Dara followed the Qadiri Sufi saint Mian Mir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, to whom Mullah Shah Badakhshi introduced him, and the mystic Sarmad Kashani, and he formed a friendship with the seventh Sikh Guru, Guru Har Rai. The treatise known as Majma-ul-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> was written in Persian in 1654–55. His Persian version of fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>, completed in 1657 and often called Sirr-i-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, proposes in its introduction that the hidden book named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> is the Upanishads. Annemarie Schimmel notes that he remained committed to the fundamentals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and repeatedly called himself a Hanafi Qadiri.</p><p>He was a patron of painting, music and dancing, tastes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> frowned upon. The album of paintings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> that bears his name was presented to his wife in 1641–42; after her death the inscriptions linking it to him were deliberately erased.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Samugarh he retreated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a>, where Shah Nawaz Khan, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, helped him raise a new army. After the rout at Deorai on 11 March 1659 he sought refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> with the Afghan chieftain Malik Jeevan, whose life he had saved more than once, but Malik Jeevan seized him and informed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, whose army took him on 10 June 1659.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> he was paraded through the streets in chains on an elephant. A convocation of nobles and clergy called by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> declared him a threat to the public peace and an apostate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and he was killed in front of his son on the night of 30 August 1659. He was buried in an unidentified grave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>'s tomb. In 2020 Sanjeev Kumar Singh, a municipal engineer, identified one of three unmarked cenotaphs beneath the dome as his, relying on the Alamgirnama; several historians and archaeologists have accepted the identification.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dara Shikoh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Princes held ranks and armies of their own and fought for the throne: Aurangzeb defeated his brother <b>Dara Shikoh</b> in 1659 and kept their father Shah Jahan a prisoner until his death in 1666.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Bundi's Raos often fought Mewar and later served the Mughals: Rao Surjan surrendered Ranthambore to Akbar in 1569, and Rao Chhatrasal was killed at Samugarh in 1658 fighting for <b>Dara Shikoh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bundi-state">Bundi State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.47,74.64&amp;year=1643" aria-label="Ajmer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6519 1192 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dara Shikoh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6969 1462h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6969 1462h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.47,74.64&amp;year=1643">What was here in 1643? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
